sections.detailed:
Discrepancies plague our lives; we do not always do what we say we will (Egan points to last year's New Year's resolutions). They keep clients stuck. The book lists the following kinds:

- what clients think or feel versus what they say;
- what they say versus what they do;
- their views of themselves versus others' views of them;
- what they are versus what they claim they want to be;
- their stated goals versus what they actually accomplish;
- their expressed values versus their actual behaviour.

Examples: Tom sees himself as witty but friends see him as biting; Minerva says fitness matters but overeats and under-exercises; George says he loves his family but is seeing another woman and stays away from home; Clarissa, unemployed for months, wants a job but will not join a retraining programme.

The Clarissa dialogue shows how to challenge a say-do discrepancy. The counsellor first reminds her that she once thought the programme was exactly what she wanted. She offers reasons (different work, long programme), and the counsellor reflects that she feels the fit is poor. Then, gently, the counsellor says she seemed so enthusiastic at first and asks what is going on. After a pause Clarissa admits she has got a bit lazy and used to being out of work. The counsellor sees she is slipping into a "culture of unemployment"; with the discrepancy now in the open, they can work together on the future she wants. The example shows challenge done gently, combining empathy with a curious, tentative invitation.
